WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 21: Project Y: The Secret City of Los Alamos

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.440
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:06.640
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:13.600
[Rebecca] High in the Jemez Mountains of northern New Mexico sits an isolated volcanic mesa overlooking the Rio Grande Valley.

4
00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:20.640
[Rebecca] In the 1930s, it was home to an elite boys' outdoor school where students rode horses and slept in open-air porches.

5
00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:32.080
[Rebecca] But in November 1942, the United States government bought the school, erected miles of barbed wire, and vanished the entire plateau behind an impenetrable wall of armed guards.

6
00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:35.760
[Rebecca] Under the codename Project Y, theoretical physicist J.

7
00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:43.440
[Rebecca] Robert Oppenheimer and General Leslie Groves gathered the greatest scientific minds on Earth to build the world's first atomic bomb.

8
00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:57.600
[Rebecca] For almost three years, thousands of people lived in a bustling city that did not appear on any map, where everyone shared the exact same mailing address, and where security was so extreme that newborn babies were officially born in a post office box.

9
00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:02.240
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story.

10
00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:23.040
[George] In university laboratories across Chicago, Berkeley, Columbia, and Princeton, the world's most brilliant physicists and chemists were racing to split the atom before Nazi Germany could construct an atomic superweapon.

11
00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:35.520
[George] However, theoretical calculations had progressed to the point where dangerous experimental physics and massive high-explosive testing required a single, unified and utterly secluded weapons laboratory.

12
00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:43.200
[George] The military director of the Manhattan Project, Brigadier General Leslie Groves, partnered with theoretical physicist J.

13
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:47.920
[George] Robert Oppenheimer to scout potential locations throughout the American West.

14
00:01:47.920 --> 00:02:02.520
[George] Oppenheimer, who owned a rustic mountain cabin in the Picos Valley and loved the New Mexico wilderness, recommended a breathtakingly isolated volcanic tabland known as the Pajarito Plateau, 7200 feet above sea level.

15
00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:16.040
[George] Perched atop steep canyon cliffs, the site was occupied only by the Los Alamos Ranch School, an exclusive boarding school where sickly boys from wealthy families learned rigorous academics and cowboy horsemanship.

16
00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:19.960
[George] Groves was instantly captivated by the strategic advantages.

17
00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:33.240
[George] Surrounded by deep ravines, the mesa could be easily defended against enemy saboteurs, while its utter geographic isolation minimized the danger of civilian casualties in case of an accidental nuclear detonation.

18
00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:44.120
[George] In November 1942, the War Department condemned 54,000 acres, giving the school headmaster and students just a few weeks to pack their bags and evacuate.

19
00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:53.320
[George] Within weeks of the school's evacuation, military construction crews transformed the serene mountain mesa into a chaotic, dust-choked boomtown.

20
00:02:53.320 --> 00:03:00.760
[George] General Groves initially envisioned a modest military research detachment of perhaps 30 scientists and 50 technicians.

21
00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:10.680
[George] But as the theoretical and engineering challenges of weaponizing uranium and plutonium expanded exponentially, the mesa exploded into a sprawling metropolis.

22
00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:29.560
[George] By 1944, more than 6,000 scientists, engineers, military police, and their families were packed into hastily constructed green wooden barracks, tarper huts, and prefabricated duplexes along unpaved streets that turned into impassable seas of mud with every spring rain.

23
00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:33.480
[George] Life atop the hill was defined by surreal contradictions.

24
00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:47.440
[George] World-renowned Nobel laureates like Niels Bauer, Enrico Fermi, Hans Beth, and Richard Feynman rubbed shoulders in the military mess hall, eating government rations on tin trays while wearing work boots and blue jeans.

25
00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:55.600
[George] Water was desperately scarce, civilian families were rationed to just a few gallons per day, and power failures were frequent.

26
00:03:55.600 --> 00:04:01.680
[George] Yet, despite the frontier hardships, the mesa buzzed with extraordinary intellectual electricity.

27
00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:19.280
[George] Scientists held chamber music recitals on grand pianos in old log cabins, organized Sunday skiing excursions into the Val Grande, and hosted boisterous Saturday night cocktail parties where Feynman played bongo drums and picked the locks of secret government safes for amusement.

28
00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:25.840
[George] The security apparatus enclosing Lowe's Alamos was the most airtight and comprehensive in American history.

29
00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:30.000
[George] To the outside world, the secret city simply did not exist.

30
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:38.320
[George] It appeared on no civilian road maps, was absent from territorial census records, and its name could never be spoken in public.

31
00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:45.680
[George] Every resident, from General Groves to the newest baby born in the military hospital, shared a single mailing address.

32
00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:46.240
[George] P.O.

33
00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:50.960
[George] Box 1663, Santa Fe, New Mexico.

34
00:04:50.960 --> 00:05:04.400
[George] All personal correspondence was screened by military censors who sat with razor blades and black ink, slicing out any mention of geography, whether, mountain topography, or scientific equipment before letters could be mailed.

35
00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:07.600
[George] Scientists were assigned aliases for travel.

36
00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:15.600
[George] Niels Bauer traveled as Nicholas Baker, Enrico Fermi became Eugene Farmer, and Oppenheimer was James Oberhelm.

37
00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:24.560
[George] Long-distance telephone calls were monitored by intelligence agents with headphones who disconnected callers instantly if forbidden code words were uttered.

38
00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:32.840
[George] Drivers' licenses issued to residents contained no names, only serial numbers and stamps from the State Motor Vehicle Department.

39
00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:43.800
[George] When babies were born at the base hospital, and there were dozens born during the project's baby boom, their official New Mexico birth certificates listed their place of birth simply as P.O.

40
00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:46.360
[George] Box 1663.

41
00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:59.560
[George] If a scientist left the mesa to visit Santa Fe, 25 miles away, they were followed by undercover military intelligence officers in plainclothes to ensure they never engaged in casual conversation with locals.

42
00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:07.480
[George] Behind the barbed wire fences of the technical area, scientists confronted an engineering puzzle of unprecedented difficulty.

43
00:06:07.480 --> 00:06:16.920
[George] When the first batches of synthetic plutonium arrived from the massive production reactors at Hanford, Washington, the physicists made a terrifying discovery.

44
00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:29.800
[George] Plutonium contained minute traces of an isotope called Plutonium-240, which underwent spontaneous fission so rapidly that a conventional gun-type assembly would predetonate and fizzle in a useless burst of heat.

45
00:06:29.800 --> 00:06:47.400
[George] The only viable method to detonate a plutonium bomb was implosion, surrounding a subcritical sphere of plutonium with an array of precision-shaped high-explosive lenses that would fire inward simultaneously within fractions of a microsecond, squeezing the metal to twice its normal density.

46
00:06:47.400 --> 00:07:02.920
[George] Oppenheimer reorganized the entire laboratory around the implosion concept, bringing in brilliant Hungarian mathematician John von Neumann to calculate shockwave hydrodynamics and chemist George Kisciakowski to cast explosive lenses in mountain bunkers.

47
00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:13.800
[George] Working 18-hour days under crushing pressure, scientists tested explosive configurations day and night, their detonations rattling the windows of Santa Fe down in the valley.

48
00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:27.040
[George] By July 1945, the prototype implosion device, affectionately nicknamed the Gadget, was assembled by hand in a stone ranch house and ready for its ultimate test in the southern New Mexico desert.

49
00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:38.320
[George] Before dawn on July 16, 1945, the culmination of Project Y took place in the desolate Jornada del Muerto Desert near Alamogordo, New Mexico.

50
00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:46.160
[George] At 5.29 in the morning, the countdown reached zero, and the gadget detonated atop a 100-foot steel tower.

51
00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:51.200
[George] The explosion released energy equivalent to 21,000 tons of TNT.

52
00:07:51.200 --> 00:08:01.440
[George] A blinding flash of light brighter than 20 midday suns illuminated the desert mountains for hundreds of miles, visible as far away as Albuquerque and El Paso.

53
00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:15.520
[George] The fireball expanded into a giant, churning column of purple, orange, and gray smoke that rose 40,000 feet into the stratosphere, melting the desert sand below into a radioactive green glass called Trinitite.

54
00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:22.080
[George] The shockwave knocked observers to the ground five miles away and shattered windows 100 miles distant.

55
00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:31.120
[George] Watching the mushroom cloud billow into the predon sky, Oppenheimer famously recalled the chilling verse from the Hindu scripture, the Bhagavad Gita.

56
00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:34.720
[George] Now I am become death, the destroyer of worlds.

57
00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:47.760
[George] The military quickly issued a cover story claiming that a massive ammunition depot had accidentally exploded in the desert, but across northern New Mexico, locals knew that something far more earth-shattering had taken place.

58
00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:58.480
[George] Three weeks later, on August 6, 1945, the atomic bomb was dropped on Hiroshima, followed three days later by the detonation over Nagasaki.

59
00:08:58.480 --> 00:08:59.840
[George] President Harry S.

60
00:09:00.280 --> 00:09:07.560
[George] Truman addressed the world by radio, announcing that American scientists had unleashed the basic power of the universe.

61
00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:13.640
[George] The next morning, the secret gates of Lowe's Alamos were suddenly front page news across the globe.

62
00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:23.240
[George] For the first time, the 6,000 residents living atop the Pajarito Plateau could tell their families what they had spent three years building in the New Mexico Mountains.

63
00:09:23.240 --> 00:09:31.800
[George] Today, Los Alamos is a premier national laboratory, still conducting cutting edge physics and supercomputing research on the mesa.

64
00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:45.480
[George] The historic log buildings of the ranch school, where Oppenheimer and Fermi walked between lab sessions, still stand along Bathtub Row, so named because they were the only houses on the mesa equipped with bathtubs during the war.

65
00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:59.160
[George] Yet the aura of Project Y remains palpable in the thin mountain air, a testament to an extraordinary gathering of genius that unlocked the atom and fundamentally changed the course of human destiny from an address that never existed.

66
00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:13.400
[George] Following the surrender of Japan in August 1945, the clandestine veil surrounding Lowe's Alamos began to slowly lift, revealing a complex scientific legacy that would define the geopolitical landscape of the modern era.

67
00:10:13.400 --> 00:10:26.120
[George] The makeshift wartime encampment was permanently reorganized under civilian control as the Lowe's Alamos National Laboratory, evolving into one of the world's premier multidisciplinary scientific institutions.

68
00:10:26.120 --> 00:10:44.680
[George] The brilliant minds who gathered on the Pajarito Plateau went on to pioneer modern computational physics, inventing the Monte Carlo method, designing the earliest electronic digital computers like Maniac Eye, and establishing the fundamental frameworks of thermonuclear power and quantum physics.

69
00:10:44.720 --> 00:10:56.640
[George] Yet for the men and women who lived atop the hill behind armed military checkpoints, the memory of Project Y remained forever stamped with moral solemnity and the intoxicating camaraderie of youth.

70
00:10:56.640 --> 00:11:07.600
[George] Today, visitors to the Mesa can walk beneath the Ponderosa pines along Bathtub Row, visiting the historic lodge where Oppenheimer met with Niels Bauer and Enrico Fermi.

71
00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:19.120
[George] The secret city that never appeared on any wartime map transformed northern New Mexico into the birthplace of the atomic age, forever altering the destiny of human civilization.

72
00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:30.240
[Rebecca] Project Wyatt Lowe's Alamos stands as one of the most astonishing scientific and logistical feats in human history, born in complete secrecy amid the ancient cliffs of northern New Mexico.

73
00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:35.920
[Rebecca] It reminds us of how human intellect and collective purpose can redefine our reality in an instant.

